#: processes/library/document-ingestion.bpmn:58
#. Diagram label
msgid "A contributor has a file for the folio"
msgstr ""

#: processes/library/document-ingestion.bpmn:29
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Accepts an upload, records its extracted metadata in the knowledge graph, and catalogues the asset in `library/` — held if materialized, referenced if not. THE BASIC FLOW, with very little process context assumed. Accept, extract the metadata into the KG, ask whether the asset is materialized, place it in library/<slug>/. It calls NO subprocess and makes no content-type decision. THE ID `Process_Ingestion` IS KEPT ON PURPOSE: `methodology-from-source.bpmn`'s Call_Ingest names it, and so does every higher-layer process that refines this flow. Refinement is by INVERSION — a refining process calls this one as its first step and continues with its own work; nothing here names a refinement, because the corpus has no BPMN extension point and a harness diagram naming a higher one would be an upward edge. The document pipeline (rungs, the four ingest phases, the L1 completeness gate, promotion, the summary queue) is such a refinement, in the layer above."
msgstr ""

#: processes/library/document-ingestion.bpmn:95
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Answered from the materialization state (`schemas/kg/materialization-state.ts`): `materialized` means the bytes are here; `referenced` means we know where they are and hold none. `unknown` is not a third branch here because a node must DECLARE its state — there is no default — and an asset this flow cannot classify is recorded as referenced-with-a-reason rather than placed as if held. Collapsing referenced into materialized is the failure the vocabulary exists to stop: corpus-grep searches library/ only, so a referenced node placed as held reads as present to every consumer."
msgstr ""

